WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.380
Configuration deployment should follow a controlled pipeline.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:12.520
Promotion from development to test, staging, and production should preserve intended security controls while allowing environment-specific values.

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:19.820
The pipeline should know which settings are ordinary configuration, which are secrets, which are generated, and which require approval.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.040
This makes configuration changes part of release management rather than a side channel.

5
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.080
Configuration drift occurs when deployed configuration changes away from the approved or expected baseline.

6
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:41.180
Drift can happen through emergency console edits, platform defaults, manual fixes, auto-scaling templates, third-party changes, or out-of-band updates.

7
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.000
Drift detection compares what is running against what should be running and highlights differences that need review.

8
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.320
Runtime configuration inventory and monitoring help teams understand production reality.

9
00:00:51.420 --> 00:01:05.580
Cloud configuration monitoring, container and Kubernetes configuration review, and platform-level visibility can detect public exposure, broad permissions, disabled logging, missing encryption, unsafe ingress, or changed security groups.

10
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:12.960
Alerting should focus on security-relevant changes rather than overwhelming teams with every harmless difference.

11
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.840
Configuration must be observable without leaking sensitive values.

12
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:25.020
Teams need to know which version, profile, and policy are active, but they should not dump secrets into logs or dashboards.

13
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.040
Rollback planning is also important.

14
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:37.540
If a configuration change breaks authentication, routing, encryption, or availability, teams should know how to restore the previous known-good state quickly and safely.
